- [Female] What/who are
you reading, watching,

00:22:49.262 --> 00:22:51.410
consuming right now

00:22:51.410 --> 00:22:53.710
just from a free time standpoint?

00:22:53.710 --> 00:22:56.460
- The people that leave
comments in my content.

00:22:56.460 --> 00:22:57.403
I consume nothing.

00:22:58.760 --> 00:23:01.095
I literally have no idea what's going on

00:23:01.095 --> 00:23:04.700
other than if it's
serendipitously hits my radar

00:23:04.700 --> 00:23:06.770
through my own community.

00:23:06.770 --> 00:23:09.760
I'm just consuming how people are reacting

00:23:09.760 --> 00:23:11.203
to what I'm focused on.

00:23:14.070 --> 00:23:16.183
And I think that's been
a huge strength of mine.

00:23:17.551 --> 00:23:20.201
For example, because I did consume a lot

00:23:20.201 --> 00:23:23.370
of Richard Pryor and Chris
Rock and Eddie Murphy as a kid,

00:23:23.370 --> 00:23:26.650
I completely believe the
way I give keynote speeches

00:23:26.650 --> 00:23:29.750
has an underlining tone
of a comedy routine

00:23:29.750 --> 00:23:32.660
more than just the classic keynote

00:23:32.660 --> 00:23:34.200
which is why I think it's worked for me

00:23:34.200 --> 00:23:35.590
'cause there's a level of entertainment

00:23:35.590 --> 00:23:38.120
that comes along when I give keynotes.

00:23:38.120 --> 00:23:39.736
So I become a character,

00:23:39.736 --> 00:23:41.882
I've been affected by them

00:23:41.882 --> 00:23:43.650
in the way that I act.

00:23:43.650 --> 00:23:46.090
I think the reason my voice is resonating

00:23:46.090 --> 00:23:49.100
is I'm not affected by
anybody else right now,

00:23:49.100 --> 00:23:52.430
I'm only affected by the way
people are reacting to me

00:23:52.430 --> 00:23:54.410
which allows me to continue to build

00:23:54.410 --> 00:23:57.440
in the purity of human truths

00:23:57.440 --> 00:24:00.203
without blending and trying to be like.

00:24:04.800 --> 00:24:08.173
- [Female] So you're
clearly a very busy guy

00:24:08.173 --> 00:24:11.060
with controlling your public image and--

00:24:11.060 --> 00:24:12.170
- You'll appreciate this,

00:24:12.170 --> 00:24:13.160
real quick on that front,

00:24:13.160 --> 00:24:14.584
just to jump in, I'll let you finish,

00:24:14.584 --> 00:24:18.633
I give zero thoughts to
controlling my public image, zero.

00:24:19.750 --> 00:24:22.030
I mean they doc, my team's in here,

00:24:22.030 --> 00:24:24.627
I don't even look at what they're making.

00:24:24.627 --> 00:24:26.910
The way I control my public image

00:24:26.910 --> 00:24:28.180
is by living my truth

00:24:28.180 --> 00:24:30.030
and letting the chips fall.

00:24:30.030 --> 00:24:31.400
It's the most liberating
thing of all time.

00:24:31.400 --> 00:24:33.406
I don't think about it at all.

00:24:33.406 --> 00:24:34.929
Keep going.

00:24:34.929 --> 00:24:37.630
- [Female] And you're
evergrowing companies,

00:24:37.630 --> 00:24:39.046
VaynerX, VaynerMedia.

00:24:39.046 --> 00:24:43.130
I'm just curious what
keeps you up at night?

00:24:43.130 --> 00:24:45.630
- Health, health.

00:24:45.630 --> 00:24:46.670
Health keeps me up at night.

00:24:46.670 --> 00:24:48.450
The health and wellbeing

00:24:48.450 --> 00:24:51.890
of the 12 people that
I'm closest related to

00:24:51.890 --> 00:24:55.163
or love the most as a friend
scares the fuck out of me,

00:24:56.710 --> 00:24:59.510
like way more than it scares most people.

00:24:59.510 --> 00:25:01.430
I think about it all the time

00:25:01.430 --> 00:25:04.603
because I don't care
about the work enough.

00:25:05.930 --> 00:25:07.540
It's what my passion is

00:25:07.540 --> 00:25:10.659
but like if a core 12 person in my life

00:25:10.659 --> 00:25:12.730
is terminally ill or passes away,

00:25:12.730 --> 00:25:14.023
it will shut me down.

00:25:15.850 --> 00:25:16.683
So that keeps me up.

00:25:16.683 --> 00:25:18.716
Professionally, I'm not kept up.

00:25:18.716 --> 00:25:20.920
I'm capable, I'm talented.

00:25:20.920 --> 00:25:22.120
Business is tricky

00:25:22.120 --> 00:25:22.953
'cause we haven't,

00:25:22.953 --> 00:25:23.786
we don't treat business

00:25:23.786 --> 00:25:26.050
the way we treat music,
artists and athletes

00:25:26.050 --> 00:25:27.863
but I think it's much more similar to that

00:25:27.863 --> 00:25:29.700
than it is other things.

00:25:29.700 --> 00:25:31.820
So like once you know
you're a good athlete,

00:25:31.820 --> 00:25:33.574
you may not win every game,

00:25:33.574 --> 00:25:36.173
but Lebron's not confused that he's bad.

00:25:37.069 --> 00:25:39.860
He doesn't think tomorrow he's
not gonna be able to play.

00:25:39.860 --> 00:25:41.780
He may not win every game,

00:25:41.780 --> 00:25:43.530
and I genuinely feel like I'm that.

00:25:44.380 --> 00:25:47.470
I'm not gonna miraculously tomorrow

00:25:47.470 --> 00:25:50.270
not be what I've been for
the last 38 years of my life,

00:25:50.270 --> 00:25:53.937
which is a disproportionately
successful business human.

00:25:53.937 --> 00:25:55.193
So I'm good.

00:25:56.912 --> 00:25:58.286
Yeah.

00:25:58.286 --> 00:25:59.690
- [Female] Hi Gary.

00:25:59.690 --> 00:26:01.940
I'm on the phone from the LA office.

00:26:01.940 --> 00:26:03.550
- I am so sorry I didn't get to say hello.

00:26:03.550 --> 00:26:04.383
I didn't know.

00:26:04.383 --> 00:26:05.823
I completely blame Alex Klein.

00:26:07.675 --> 00:26:09.157
Who is it?

00:26:09.157 --> 00:26:10.240
Please go around,

00:26:10.240 --> 00:26:11.318
I don't know how many are on the phone

00:26:11.318 --> 00:26:12.900
but please say hello.

00:26:12.900 --> 00:26:14.217
Go ahead.

00:26:14.217 --> 00:26:15.050
- [Lindsey] Okay.

00:26:15.050 --> 00:26:15.883
So I'm Lindsey,

00:26:15.883 --> 00:26:16.716
I'm from the LA office

00:26:16.716 --> 00:26:18.144
and I'm actually from Atlanta, Georgia

00:26:18.144 --> 00:26:19.350
but (mumbles).

00:26:19.350 --> 00:26:21.450
- Awesome, Linds, go ahead.

00:26:21.450 --> 00:26:22.950
- [Lindsey] So I just
had a follow-up question

00:26:22.950 --> 00:26:24.370
when you were talking
about social media earlier

00:26:24.370 --> 00:26:25.203
and exposing people.

00:26:25.203 --> 00:26:28.310
What's your opinion on Instagram passing,

00:26:28.310 --> 00:26:30.550
taking away likes and stuff like that?

00:26:30.550 --> 00:26:31.550
- I hope they do it.

00:26:33.210 --> 00:26:36.153
- [Lindsey] How will
it impact social media?

00:26:36.153 --> 00:26:38.770
- It will change a lot
of people's creative

00:26:38.770 --> 00:26:40.330
because they won't pander to likes.

00:26:40.330 --> 00:26:41.520
They'll start putting out more

00:26:41.520 --> 00:26:43.640
of what they want to actually put out.

00:26:43.640 --> 00:26:48.640
It will make a very high
percentage of people lose leverage

00:26:48.703 --> 00:26:50.980
because their social equity

00:26:50.980 --> 00:26:53.869
is predicated on their following count.

00:26:53.869 --> 00:26:56.343
And I think it would
lead to good behavior.

00:26:57.360 --> 00:26:58.193
- [Lindsey] So specifically,

00:26:58.193 --> 00:27:00.270
how do you think it will affect
your social media presence?

00:27:00.270 --> 00:27:01.407
- Zero.

00:27:01.407 --> 00:27:02.630
- [Lindsey] Zero?

00:27:02.630 --> 00:27:03.463
- Zero.

00:27:04.535 --> 00:27:07.660
I'm not predicated on my following count

00:27:07.660 --> 00:27:08.666
or my blue check

00:27:08.666 --> 00:27:11.000
or how many likes a post gets.

00:27:11.000 --> 00:27:13.220
I'm predicated on the
message I'm delivering

00:27:13.220 --> 00:27:14.053
in the content.

00:27:14.986 --> 00:27:17.160
- [Lindsey] Cool, thanks.

00:27:17.160 --> 00:27:18.445
- You're welcome.

00:27:18.445 --> 00:27:20.930
- [Female] I kind of have a
follow-up comment on that.

00:27:20.930 --> 00:27:22.950
I was reading an article about TikTok

00:27:22.950 --> 00:27:26.923
and basically the article
said people on TikTok,

00:27:26.923 --> 00:27:29.300
their likes are like their value,

00:27:29.300 --> 00:27:30.800
their likes are their money,

00:27:30.800 --> 00:27:32.584
their likes are their--

00:27:32.584 --> 00:27:33.417
- It's their currency.

00:27:33.417 --> 00:27:35.411
- It's their currency.
- Yes.

00:27:35.411 --> 00:27:37.760
And I just sat back and thought

00:27:37.760 --> 00:27:40.360
and I was like what kind
of world would we live in

00:27:40.360 --> 00:27:43.290
if that's the only thing that we value?

00:27:43.290 --> 00:27:44.335
We sit home and we go,

00:27:44.335 --> 00:27:46.520
oh hey, guess how many likes I got today?

00:27:46.520 --> 00:27:49.700
- It would be the world
we have always lived in.

00:27:49.700 --> 00:27:52.317
Let me tell you what likes were in 1984,

00:27:52.317 --> 00:27:54.663
your popularity rank in your high school.

00:27:56.341 --> 00:27:58.363
This is human behavior.

00:27:59.874 --> 00:28:02.300
- [Female] And then another question is

00:28:02.300 --> 00:28:04.150
do you think that your feelings

00:28:04.150 --> 00:28:06.130
and your relationship with your family

00:28:06.130 --> 00:28:08.327
reflects in your work
and your business life

00:28:08.327 --> 00:28:09.820
and the model at Vayner?

00:28:09.820 --> 00:28:11.560
- Yes, 100%.

00:28:11.560 --> 00:28:15.850
I think that this company is a
complete and utter reflection

00:28:15.850 --> 00:28:17.570
of the way I was parented

00:28:17.570 --> 00:28:21.000
and the circumstances
in which I was parented.

00:28:21.000 --> 00:28:21.940
100%.

00:28:21.940 --> 00:28:26.940
I think that this company
is optimistically practical.

00:28:31.010 --> 00:28:33.940
And that's uniquely what
I can bring to the table

00:28:33.940 --> 00:28:34.773
in this company.

00:28:34.773 --> 00:28:37.110
So this company's extremely
young and coastal,

00:28:37.110 --> 00:28:39.467
which means it's
unbelievably social, liberal,

00:28:39.467 --> 00:28:42.000
which is incredible 'cause I am as well

00:28:42.000 --> 00:28:44.649
but that also leads to vulnerability.

00:28:44.649 --> 00:28:48.590
And so my practicality
in immigrant upbringing

00:28:48.590 --> 00:28:50.500
and merit-based infrastructure

00:28:50.500 --> 00:28:51.470
helps us a lot

00:28:51.470 --> 00:28:53.033
when the most senior leaders

00:28:53.033 --> 00:28:55.580
do things that are currently popular

00:28:55.580 --> 00:28:56.890
but are unbelievably

00:28:58.313 --> 00:29:02.594
scary to me of what that
could lead to a month later.

00:29:02.594 --> 00:29:06.117
I mean Slack at VaynerMedia

00:29:06.117 --> 00:29:08.603
is like dark Reddit.

00:29:10.681 --> 00:29:11.940
There's a lot of good

00:29:12.990 --> 00:29:14.538
but there's plenty of dark

00:29:14.538 --> 00:29:17.127
and that's just the nature of what happens

00:29:17.127 --> 00:29:19.100
especially when what's happening

00:29:19.100 --> 00:29:20.950
outside the four walls of VaynerMedia

00:29:20.950 --> 00:29:22.212
is happening in our society

00:29:22.212 --> 00:29:25.740
which is activating people
in a completely different way

00:29:25.740 --> 00:29:28.700
than a decade ago or two decades ago

00:29:28.700 --> 00:29:30.150
or two decades from today.

00:29:30.150 --> 00:29:33.500
And so yes, I feel like

00:29:33.500 --> 00:29:35.930
I'm very fortunate to
be the human that I am

00:29:35.930 --> 00:29:37.410
during this time

00:29:37.410 --> 00:29:41.230
because I think I have practical optimism.

00:29:41.230 --> 00:29:45.560
I'm a Jets fan because in August of 1982

00:29:45.560 --> 00:29:47.279
when I moved to Edison, New Jersey,

00:29:47.279 --> 00:29:50.910
what 1982 kids did was you went outside

00:29:50.910 --> 00:29:52.520
and randomly walked around.

00:29:52.520 --> 00:29:54.590
And in one of those walking arounds

00:29:54.590 --> 00:29:56.846
I found three kids
throwing a Nerf football

00:29:56.846 --> 00:29:59.310
and a kid by the name of Eric Godfrey

00:29:59.310 --> 00:30:00.477
looked me dead in the face and said,

00:30:00.477 --> 00:30:01.804
"Who do you like?"

00:30:01.804 --> 00:30:03.310
I don't recall what happens next

00:30:03.310 --> 00:30:05.230
but I remember who do you like in football

00:30:05.230 --> 00:30:06.590
but basically he's like

00:30:06.590 --> 00:30:07.460
well we're Jet fans,

00:30:07.460 --> 00:30:09.350
so you're a Jets fan

00:30:09.350 --> 00:30:12.720
and that September I
started watching the Jets

00:30:12.720 --> 00:30:14.430
and I haven't stopped.

00:30:14.430 --> 00:30:15.320
That is it,

00:30:15.320 --> 00:30:17.023
that is the story.

00:30:19.299 --> 00:30:22.200
- I'm curious what you
said earlier about tasting,

00:30:22.200 --> 00:30:23.921
tasting things in your 20s.

00:30:23.921 --> 00:30:27.510
Where would you say is
the best spot to do that?

00:30:27.510 --> 00:30:28.610
Is it at a company?

00:30:28.610 --> 00:30:30.436
Is it in college?

00:30:30.436 --> 00:30:31.900
- It's definitely not in college.

00:30:31.900 --> 00:30:34.057
- Taking the trip to
Argentina or something?

00:30:34.057 --> 00:30:37.041
- It's anything but college only because,

00:30:37.041 --> 00:30:39.120
and not to overly razz on college

00:30:39.120 --> 00:30:41.130
but it's a fake environment.

00:30:41.130 --> 00:30:42.230
There's nothing about college

00:30:42.230 --> 00:30:44.693
that has anything to do with
your professional career.

00:30:45.778 --> 00:30:48.450
So not college

00:30:48.450 --> 00:30:50.850
but then anything in the real world.

00:30:50.850 --> 00:30:52.390
In a company that has flexibility

00:30:52.390 --> 00:30:53.920
to let you do a lot of
different disciplines,

00:30:53.920 --> 00:30:55.400
something I'm proud of that we have here,

00:30:55.400 --> 00:30:56.550
and I'm sure others do.

00:30:58.109 --> 00:31:01.500
I believe the framework
of the world is backwards.

00:31:01.500 --> 00:31:03.780
I think that everybody in their 20s

00:31:03.780 --> 00:31:06.085
should be at their highest risk behavior.

00:31:06.085 --> 00:31:09.940
And I think most of you
are feeling pressure

00:31:09.940 --> 00:31:11.150
on the other side

00:31:11.150 --> 00:31:14.340
to start being more
responsible in real life,

00:31:14.340 --> 00:31:15.710
that we've created this narrative

00:31:15.710 --> 00:31:17.780
that you fuck around for
four years in college

00:31:17.780 --> 00:31:19.110
and it's your last great thing

00:31:19.110 --> 00:31:21.970
and then in May when you're 22,

00:31:21.970 --> 00:31:23.420
you now need to get serious

00:31:23.420 --> 00:31:25.490
and everything becomes super practical

00:31:25.490 --> 00:31:26.759
both as a macro thing,

00:31:26.759 --> 00:31:29.870
a level of underlining pressure
we feel from our parents,

00:31:29.870 --> 00:31:31.990
an underlining pressure
we feel from ourselves

00:31:31.990 --> 00:31:34.610
to prove that we are what we think we are

00:31:34.610 --> 00:31:36.268
and I think that unfortunately,

00:31:36.268 --> 00:31:38.610
I'm trying to figure out
through my communication

00:31:38.610 --> 00:31:41.390
and hopefully that influences
others to start a debate,

00:31:41.390 --> 00:31:43.690
I think the right model is actually

00:31:43.690 --> 00:31:46.451
that 20 to 30 should be ludicrous,

00:31:46.451 --> 00:31:48.850
should be all the most extreme versions

00:31:48.850 --> 00:31:50.150
of four different countries,

00:31:50.150 --> 00:31:51.623
19 different jobs,

00:31:52.837 --> 00:31:56.872
that you have the ability
still at that point

00:31:56.872 --> 00:31:59.963
to have the humility to live shitty

00:31:59.963 --> 00:32:02.270
which then gives you the luxury

00:32:02.270 --> 00:32:03.453
to be able to taste.

00:32:04.380 --> 00:32:06.310
When you're 36,

00:32:06.310 --> 00:32:08.159
you now have created overhead

00:32:08.159 --> 00:32:11.980
in family, home, car, loans,

00:32:11.980 --> 00:32:13.280
mortgages and other things

00:32:13.280 --> 00:32:14.983
that give you less flexibility.

00:32:16.050 --> 00:32:16.900
When you're 22,

00:32:16.900 --> 00:32:18.180
it still seem feasible

00:32:18.180 --> 00:32:19.637
to live with five other girls

00:32:19.637 --> 00:32:21.430
in a two bedroom apartment

00:32:21.430 --> 00:32:23.833
where everyone's trying
to figure out their world.

00:32:26.459 --> 00:32:28.300
- Yeah and then college,

00:32:28.300 --> 00:32:31.810
you don't like college purely
because of the value prop?

00:32:31.810 --> 00:32:33.620
- I don like college for the value prop

00:32:33.620 --> 00:32:35.870
for a big percentage of people.

00:32:35.870 --> 00:32:37.020
I think college works for,

00:32:37.020 --> 00:32:39.660
somebody left a comment
yesterday on my Instagram

00:32:39.660 --> 00:32:42.950
that like GaryVee, you
convinced me to stay in school

00:32:42.950 --> 00:32:44.520
and I was pumped.

00:32:44.520 --> 00:32:46.430
And I was like yes,

00:32:46.430 --> 00:32:47.800
this kid is hearing me.

00:32:47.800 --> 00:32:49.136
I'm not shitting on college.

00:32:49.136 --> 00:32:51.943
I'm cheerleading for self awareness.

00:32:54.169 --> 00:32:57.593
There's no such thing as one
thing works for everyone.

00:33:00.339 --> 00:33:04.140
And one of the scariest
things in American society,

00:33:04.140 --> 00:33:05.950
one of the scariest things

00:33:05.950 --> 00:33:08.740
is parents putting pressure
on kids to go to school

00:33:08.740 --> 00:33:10.520
to make the parent feel good

00:33:10.520 --> 00:33:13.050
and making the kid take on debt for that

00:33:13.050 --> 00:33:15.305
when the kid doesn't feel
like they want to do that

00:33:15.305 --> 00:33:17.850
and then they come out the other side

00:33:17.850 --> 00:33:19.860
and they literally spend the next 15 years

00:33:19.860 --> 00:33:21.453
trying to reconcile that debt.

00:33:25.940 --> 00:33:28.140
That's why you have a
lot of unhappy people.

00:33:29.400 --> 00:33:30.500
And then what that means

00:33:30.500 --> 00:33:31.660
is then they go yeah,

00:33:31.660 --> 00:33:32.680
I'm gonna vote for the person

00:33:32.680 --> 00:33:34.830
that's gonna wipe out college debt, right,

00:33:34.830 --> 00:33:36.180
'cause that's your short term issue,

00:33:36.180 --> 00:33:38.330
just like other people
vote to the republican side

00:33:38.330 --> 00:33:40.250
on their short term issue

00:33:40.250 --> 00:33:43.127
which then leads to accountability issues

00:33:43.127 --> 00:33:45.430
which leads into people like

00:33:45.430 --> 00:33:46.680
Google needs to take care of me.

00:33:46.680 --> 00:33:48.680
This is how the machines are gonna win.

00:33:48.680 --> 00:33:49.980
You understand that right?

00:33:51.700 --> 00:33:53.350
I don't want the machines to win.

00:33:54.456 --> 00:33:57.810
I want all of you to think
every single thing is your fault

00:33:57.810 --> 00:34:01.980
without judging yourself
into depression which,

00:34:01.980 --> 00:34:02.910
everything's your fault,

00:34:02.910 --> 00:34:04.010
that's a great thing,

00:34:04.010 --> 00:34:05.140
means you're in control,

00:34:05.140 --> 00:34:05.973
let's go.

00:34:11.343 --> 00:34:15.260
- So this actually is a good
segway to this question I have.

00:34:15.260 --> 00:34:16.970
So for someone like myself

00:34:16.970 --> 00:34:19.110
who wants to go into
entrepreneurship ultimately

00:34:19.110 --> 00:34:20.245
and start a business,

00:34:20.245 --> 00:34:22.210
I'm interested in several areas

00:34:22.210 --> 00:34:24.340
like media, emerging tech, all that

00:34:24.340 --> 00:34:25.305
but I don't really know

00:34:25.305 --> 00:34:27.710
which specific thing I want to do,

00:34:27.710 --> 00:34:31.310
would you say to like
start at some company first

00:34:31.310 --> 00:34:33.150
and then kind of figure it out from there

00:34:33.150 --> 00:34:34.440
or just like kind of go--

00:34:34.440 --> 00:34:35.668
- How do you learn?

00:34:35.668 --> 00:34:38.080
- I think mostly by doing.

00:34:38.080 --> 00:34:39.020
- So then don't

00:34:40.334 --> 00:34:41.720
go to a company.

00:34:41.720 --> 00:34:44.259
- But I feel like there's a lot of like,

00:34:44.259 --> 00:34:47.825
I guess pressure in a sense to go--

00:34:47.825 --> 00:34:49.875
- You can stop this sentence right there.

00:34:50.760 --> 00:34:52.970
You know that you learn that way

00:34:52.970 --> 00:34:56.690
but you're gonna do something
else based on somebody else.

00:34:56.690 --> 00:34:58.683
- Yeah, that makes sense.

00:35:00.045 --> 00:35:02.220
- That's what everyone's doing, bro.

00:35:02.220 --> 00:35:03.450
And so everyone's sitting around

00:35:03.450 --> 00:35:05.561
and trying to figure out why everybody,

00:35:05.561 --> 00:35:08.420
all these young people are so upset,

00:35:08.420 --> 00:35:10.469
it's because of all this.

00:35:10.469 --> 00:35:13.800
'Cause they were overcoddled

00:35:13.800 --> 00:35:16.714
by a generation of overcoddling parents.

00:35:16.714 --> 00:35:19.364
And they don't know how to
fucking do their own shit.

00:35:21.130 --> 00:35:23.160
And I think they can do tons of shit

00:35:23.160 --> 00:35:24.829
and believe you can do so much

00:35:24.829 --> 00:35:26.313
as long as you tune out.

00:35:28.150 --> 00:35:30.210
Are you in a place where
you're comfortable to do

00:35:30.210 --> 00:35:31.620
what I lived through?

00:35:31.620 --> 00:35:32.500
I mean I lived it.

00:35:32.500 --> 00:35:34.754
I lived the world where

00:35:34.754 --> 00:35:36.322
now I'm me,

00:35:36.322 --> 00:35:38.682
but 26 year old me

00:35:38.682 --> 00:35:43.330
was ringing up my friends from high school

00:35:43.330 --> 00:35:45.900
at my dad's liquor store

00:35:47.430 --> 00:35:50.380
when they rolled in with their BMW

00:35:50.380 --> 00:35:52.660
because they went to a good school

00:35:52.660 --> 00:35:54.380
and started working at Goldman Sachs

00:35:54.380 --> 00:35:55.620
and could afford one,

00:35:55.620 --> 00:35:56.453
or not really,

00:35:56.453 --> 00:35:58.460
but they wanted to look like they could,

00:35:58.460 --> 00:36:02.520
and they were buying
a case of Moet Chandon

00:36:02.520 --> 00:36:05.040
and I was ringing it up for them,

00:36:05.040 --> 00:36:06.320
and this is real stories now,

00:36:06.320 --> 00:36:09.010
and carrying the case to their car

00:36:09.010 --> 00:36:10.630
and putting it in their trunk.

00:36:10.630 --> 00:36:12.200
I walked in feeling fulfilled

00:36:12.200 --> 00:36:14.142
because I was happy I made a sale.

00:36:14.142 --> 00:36:17.510
They were leaving there
feeling unbelievable

00:36:17.510 --> 00:36:21.970
because Gary's bringing out my case.

00:36:21.970 --> 00:36:25.520
The problem was that was
just when we were 26.

00:36:25.520 --> 00:36:26.980
Now that we're 43,

00:36:26.980 --> 00:36:28.603
it's real, real, real different.

00:36:30.190 --> 00:36:32.110
I just had the internal strength

00:36:32.110 --> 00:36:33.950
to deal with that scenario.

00:36:33.950 --> 00:36:35.523
99% of you aren't.

00:36:42.060 --> 00:36:43.270
- [Male] How fucking great do you feel

00:36:43.270 --> 00:36:44.370
at the end of the day?

00:36:46.400 --> 00:36:48.280
- Really fucking great.

00:36:48.280 --> 00:36:49.700
Mainly though,

00:36:49.700 --> 00:36:51.610
but I felt really, really great at 24

00:36:51.610 --> 00:36:54.732
when I schlept that case
into my friend's car

00:36:54.732 --> 00:36:57.280
because I love process,

00:36:57.280 --> 00:36:59.370
not winning.

00:36:59.370 --> 00:37:00.750
I actually like losing more than winning,

00:37:00.750 --> 00:37:01.670
I've come to learn.

00:37:01.670 --> 00:37:03.770
This is something I'm
really trying to reconcile.

00:37:03.770 --> 00:37:06.650
I actually like losing more than winning.

00:37:06.650 --> 00:37:08.350
It speaks volumes to why I'm obsessed

00:37:08.350 --> 00:37:09.880
with the Jets and Knicks

00:37:09.880 --> 00:37:13.110
and completely have abandoned
the Yankees and the Rangers

00:37:13.110 --> 00:37:14.710
after they won a championship.

00:37:14.710 --> 00:37:17.163
I like losing more.

00:37:18.590 --> 00:37:20.261
- Micro-losing or macro?

00:37:20.261 --> 00:37:21.560
- Micro-losing.

00:37:21.560 --> 00:37:23.420
But everything is micro-losing

00:37:23.420 --> 00:37:25.093
besides death or bankruptcy.

00:37:26.600 --> 00:37:27.585
- [Female] Like being the underdog?

00:37:27.585 --> 00:37:28.668
- Yep.

00:37:28.668 --> 00:37:31.553
Like I just don't give a
fuck what you guys think.

00:37:33.060 --> 00:37:35.660
And that goes from you all
the way up to my parents.

00:37:36.560 --> 00:37:37.960
- [Female] How?

00:37:37.960 --> 00:37:40.040
- By recognizing that you guys don't have,

00:37:40.040 --> 00:37:42.260
nor my parents has all the context.

00:37:42.260 --> 00:37:43.773
Nobody fully knows you.

00:37:48.266 --> 00:37:50.743
It's actually through empathy.

00:37:53.056 --> 00:37:54.222
- [Male] And what age was that like?

00:37:54.222 --> 00:37:55.433
- Early.

00:37:56.510 --> 00:37:57.760
Phew, early.

00:37:57.760 --> 00:37:58.760
I can't believe how I,

00:37:58.760 --> 00:38:00.720
now understanding a little bit more,

00:38:00.720 --> 00:38:02.764
back to being in your own cocoon,

00:38:02.764 --> 00:38:06.518
I can't believe the mentality
I brought into high school.

00:38:06.518 --> 00:38:08.830
To navigate high school the way I did,

00:38:08.830 --> 00:38:11.350
which is like completely and utterly

00:38:11.350 --> 00:38:13.744
not affected by peer pressure in hindsight

00:38:13.744 --> 00:38:15.490
is wild to me.

00:38:15.490 --> 00:38:17.610
That takes a level of self confidence

00:38:17.610 --> 00:38:20.040
at such an early age that I think,

00:38:20.040 --> 00:38:22.313
I just understand so much
more of what I am now

00:38:22.313 --> 00:38:24.010
really on that window

00:38:24.010 --> 00:38:26.800
'cause that is such a tough spot and

00:38:29.899 --> 00:38:30.732
that.

00:38:30.732 --> 00:38:32.960
- [Male] Do you attribute
that to your parents

00:38:32.960 --> 00:38:34.400
and the work from a young age or?

00:38:34.400 --> 00:38:35.233
- Yes.

00:38:36.140 --> 00:38:37.632
The DNA my parents gave me,

00:38:37.632 --> 00:38:40.713
clearly the way that my mother raised me,

00:38:41.771 --> 00:38:44.403
having to earn my keep

00:38:44.403 --> 00:38:46.950
and then just DNA man,

00:38:46.950 --> 00:38:48.850
just like that voice that we all have.

00:38:50.960 --> 00:38:53.110
I just always felt that I was better

00:38:53.110 --> 00:38:55.760
and here's where it gets really good,

00:38:55.760 --> 00:38:57.990
but if the world showed me I wasn't,

00:38:57.990 --> 00:38:59.370
I remember going through,

00:38:59.370 --> 00:39:00.203
this is actually interesting.

00:39:00.203 --> 00:39:01.380
I don't know if I've ever shared this.

00:39:01.380 --> 00:39:05.800
I remember vividly going
through the ages of seven to 16

00:39:05.800 --> 00:39:09.973
being stunned any time I
didn't win in something,

00:39:11.020 --> 00:39:12.513
like shocked,

00:39:14.690 --> 00:39:19.300
like just could not believe
that Rick was better at tennis

00:39:19.300 --> 00:39:21.430
or John just beat me in one on one

00:39:21.430 --> 00:39:24.490
or I wasn't the fastest in my class,

00:39:24.490 --> 00:39:26.023
just shocked.

00:39:26.970 --> 00:39:28.690
And I've been thinking about that lately

00:39:28.690 --> 00:39:30.770
'cause it sparked in me a few months ago,

00:39:30.770 --> 00:39:32.900
I'm like, wait a minute, that
was weird, what was that?

00:39:32.900 --> 00:39:34.560
'Cause I just kind of remembered it,

00:39:34.560 --> 00:39:35.393
like shocked,

00:39:35.393 --> 00:39:37.090
I remember actually

00:39:37.090 --> 00:39:41.100
what seemed in my mind as a
disappointing thing at the time

00:39:41.100 --> 00:39:42.710
and now I look at it as a great thing,

00:39:42.710 --> 00:39:46.280
I remember thinking
somewhere around 13, 14, 15,

00:39:46.280 --> 00:39:48.350
that I lost my expectation

00:39:48.350 --> 00:39:50.793
of always winning in
everything every time.

00:39:51.730 --> 00:39:54.380
And I remember thinking
like that wasn't good

00:39:54.380 --> 00:39:56.230
but in hindsight I think it was great

00:39:56.230 --> 00:39:58.580
because the reality is
I got into this place

00:39:58.580 --> 00:40:01.700
of complete and utter confidence

00:40:01.700 --> 00:40:04.400
but being able to not come up with excuses

00:40:04.400 --> 00:40:05.500
if it didn't work out,

00:40:06.690 --> 00:40:08.410
like watching people lose

00:40:08.410 --> 00:40:10.083
and then blame the sun,

00:40:11.229 --> 00:40:15.131
literally, I overheard it a
couple weeks ago somewhere,

00:40:15.131 --> 00:40:16.741
I was walking,

00:40:16.741 --> 00:40:19.005
oh, on vacation, I overheard it,

00:40:19.005 --> 00:40:22.320
this kid lost to his
cousin, it seemed to me,

00:40:22.320 --> 00:40:23.522
they didn't look like brothers

00:40:23.522 --> 00:40:26.030
and he was blaming the sun.

00:40:26.030 --> 00:40:27.611
And it took everything I had

00:40:27.611 --> 00:40:29.720
to not like roll in and be like bro,

00:40:29.720 --> 00:40:32.690
I love you but like you need to realize

00:40:32.690 --> 00:40:34.800
the sun was there for him too.

00:40:34.800 --> 00:40:37.268
And I think a lot about
that, accountability.

00:40:37.268 --> 00:40:40.481
If you're coming up with excuses,

00:40:40.481 --> 00:40:42.553
being super confident

00:40:42.553 --> 00:40:44.920
filled with excuses
when it doesn't work out

00:40:44.920 --> 00:40:46.083
is called delusion.

00:40:48.200 --> 00:40:52.523
So I feel great 'cause I love process.

00:40:55.636 --> 00:40:57.250
- [Female] What kind of impact

00:40:57.250 --> 00:40:58.570
do you want to leave on the world?

00:40:58.570 --> 00:41:01.010
Obviously a successful
business is one of them

00:41:01.010 --> 00:41:03.540
but it seems to me that you talk a lot

00:41:03.540 --> 00:41:05.800
about personal care and empathy

00:41:05.800 --> 00:41:08.207
which is really refreshing
and nice to hear.

00:41:08.207 --> 00:41:09.770
I want to know what your impact

00:41:09.770 --> 00:41:12.423
and what you want to leave before you go.

00:41:13.910 --> 00:41:18.910
- I want to be the greatest
entrepreneur of all time

00:41:19.295 --> 00:41:21.953
by reframing what an entrepreneur is.

00:41:23.752 --> 00:41:28.752
And so I probably have a very
ideological point of view.

00:41:29.898 --> 00:41:31.490
It was crazy when you just said,

00:41:31.490 --> 00:41:33.890
obviously, leave a successful business

00:41:33.890 --> 00:41:35.930
and it was funny how my chemicals reacted,

00:41:35.930 --> 00:41:37.693
which was like not really.

00:41:39.010 --> 00:41:39.843
I'm into it,

00:41:39.843 --> 00:41:41.180
I love it, it's my sport.

00:41:41.180 --> 00:41:43.515
It's also why I garage sale.

00:41:43.515 --> 00:41:45.490
I just like the game.

00:41:45.490 --> 00:41:47.260
So sure, but

00:41:48.260 --> 00:41:50.390
if you read my DMs for a day,

00:41:50.390 --> 00:41:55.390
you'd understand that I'm
way more evolved into that.

00:41:55.560 --> 00:41:57.160
And I think people are
sensing it, to your point.

00:41:57.160 --> 00:42:00.280
And I just want to
leave a positive impact.

00:42:00.280 --> 00:42:03.051
Look, I think I have a charisma
and communication level

00:42:03.051 --> 00:42:06.047
that allows me to have impact.

00:42:06.047 --> 00:42:08.220
And I think when one is gifted

00:42:08.220 --> 00:42:10.150
with that extreme of that capability

00:42:10.150 --> 00:42:12.300
they can go one of two ways.

00:42:12.300 --> 00:42:14.900
And I'd like to go the way

00:42:14.900 --> 00:42:18.090
that history tends to look
positive on, not negative.

00:42:18.090 --> 00:42:20.480
I'm gonna leave a lot
of money on the table

00:42:20.480 --> 00:42:21.982
and a lot of personal gain

00:42:21.982 --> 00:42:25.390
for a really nice reputation

00:42:25.390 --> 00:42:28.513
when I can't even taste
it 'cause I'll be gone.

00:42:29.950 --> 00:42:31.970
I want two days of trending on Twitter,

00:42:31.970 --> 00:42:32.993
not two hours.

00:42:35.600 --> 00:42:38.160
- [Female] Do you have a
definition of success for you?

00:42:38.160 --> 00:42:38.993
- I do.

00:42:38.993 --> 00:42:40.020
- 'Cause I know that,

00:42:40.020 --> 00:42:43.980
I feel like your businesses
and everything's always growing

00:42:43.980 --> 00:42:46.320
and moving forward and
looking into the future

00:42:46.320 --> 00:42:47.460
and what's the next thing.

00:42:47.460 --> 00:42:48.666
So is there ever like,

00:42:48.666 --> 00:42:51.426
or is it like a step process?

00:42:51.426 --> 00:42:53.700
- My definition of success for me

00:42:53.700 --> 00:42:55.890
is to be able to do what I want to do

00:42:55.890 --> 00:42:58.283
at all times always and be happy about it.

00:42:59.240 --> 00:43:00.743
Having freedom is everything.

00:43:02.260 --> 00:43:03.905
- Has that changed over the years?

00:43:03.905 --> 00:43:05.010
- No

00:43:05.010 --> 00:43:06.240
but I don't think I was,

00:43:06.240 --> 00:43:09.400
I didn't realize what
was going on in my youth.

00:43:09.400 --> 00:43:10.518
You know what I mean?
- Yeah.

00:43:10.518 --> 00:43:12.495
- So no I wasn't,

00:43:12.495 --> 00:43:14.970
it's hard for you to really
know yourself completely

00:43:14.970 --> 00:43:15.840
at this point.

00:43:15.840 --> 00:43:16.980
You'll look back and realize

00:43:16.980 --> 00:43:18.200
it was all the same shit

00:43:18.200 --> 00:43:21.230
but articulating it takes
time within yourself

00:43:21.230 --> 00:43:22.290
and also it just takes time

00:43:22.290 --> 00:43:24.730
of you actually executing on it.

00:43:24.730 --> 00:43:26.580
Back to the subcontext

00:43:26.580 --> 00:43:27.500
of what we're talking about here,

00:43:27.500 --> 00:43:28.850
one of my biggest problems right now

00:43:28.850 --> 00:43:31.870
of everybody whose trying
to make positive impact

00:43:31.870 --> 00:43:34.773
is they're doing semantical
work, not actual work.

00:43:36.582 --> 00:43:40.300
They're jockeying for
their personal reputation

00:43:40.300 --> 00:43:42.810
by what they wear or
what hashtag they use,

00:43:42.810 --> 00:43:44.950
not necessarily what
they're actually doing.

00:43:44.950 --> 00:43:46.450
And if they actually asked themselves,

00:43:46.450 --> 00:43:47.760
once they actually embrace

00:43:47.760 --> 00:43:49.720
the fact that they're being selfish,

00:43:49.720 --> 00:43:51.830
then they can become selfless.

00:43:51.830 --> 00:43:54.220
When they're posturing to selflessness

00:43:54.220 --> 00:43:57.330
and disguising their true selfishness,

00:43:57.330 --> 00:43:58.370
they're losing

00:43:58.370 --> 00:43:59.500
'cause that will play out.

00:43:59.500 --> 00:44:02.383
And that is the vulnerability
of being overwoke.

00:44:04.820 --> 00:44:06.070
That's the vulnerability.

00:44:07.745 --> 00:44:08.578
And these are,

00:44:08.578 --> 00:44:10.349
and the reason I like saying them out loud

00:44:10.349 --> 00:44:13.240
is because people aren't
saying that part out loud

00:44:13.240 --> 00:44:15.350
which then doesn't
allow a lot of this good

00:44:15.350 --> 00:44:16.683
to actually be effective.

00:44:18.200 --> 00:44:20.470
And then it starts getting into delusion.

00:44:20.470 --> 00:44:22.920
There's so much I love about

00:44:24.020 --> 00:44:26.300
making the world a better place

00:44:26.300 --> 00:44:27.723
but it has to be practical.

00:44:29.235 --> 00:44:32.210
I would be thrilled if
the government decides

00:44:32.210 --> 00:44:34.610
to wipe out all these college debts

00:44:34.610 --> 00:44:37.150
but let me promise you what the
reaction to that's gonna be,

00:44:37.150 --> 00:44:39.110
even worse behavior.

00:44:39.110 --> 00:44:41.960
Watch what the 27 year old does

00:44:41.960 --> 00:44:44.336
when you take 230 off her bill.

00:44:44.336 --> 00:44:47.460
Watch what she then does
for the next 20 years.

00:44:47.460 --> 00:44:48.840
It's not gonna be as good as people think.

00:44:48.840 --> 00:44:51.420
Recognizing that there's
always gonna be some level

00:44:51.420 --> 00:44:53.210
of like whichever one you do,

00:44:53.210 --> 00:44:54.394
you have to know yourself.

00:44:54.394 --> 00:44:55.751
What I know about myself

00:44:55.751 --> 00:45:00.084
is I don't have microregret behavior.

00:45:00.084 --> 00:45:02.840
Maybe when I'm much, much,
much older it will kick in

00:45:02.840 --> 00:45:03.895
'cause I'll run out of time

00:45:03.895 --> 00:45:06.000
but having time in front of me

00:45:06.000 --> 00:45:08.810
doesn't allow me to dwell or regret.

00:45:08.810 --> 00:45:11.190
Other people I'm watching here are 24

00:45:11.190 --> 00:45:12.880
and are regretting shit already.

00:45:12.880 --> 00:45:14.190
I'm like okay,

00:45:14.190 --> 00:45:15.600
I need to help them think about this

00:45:15.600 --> 00:45:17.220
because that's a bad framework

00:45:17.220 --> 00:45:19.560
because if you regret,

00:45:19.560 --> 00:45:21.420
if you're that insecure,

00:45:21.420 --> 00:45:23.575
if that's your perspective,

00:45:23.575 --> 00:45:25.484
then you have no right answer

00:45:25.484 --> 00:45:28.622
'cause if you go and travel and you're 34,

00:45:28.622 --> 00:45:31.710
whatever you are financially
and professionally at 34,

00:45:31.710 --> 00:45:33.090
you're gonna be like, mother fucker,

00:45:33.090 --> 00:45:35.353
I should've worked more, fuck Argentina.

00:45:36.401 --> 00:45:39.340
Or if you do the other thing
and you work your face off,

00:45:39.340 --> 00:45:40.640
you'll be like fuck,

00:45:40.640 --> 00:45:42.140
I ended up just like fucking Gary,

00:45:42.140 --> 00:45:43.370
all my friends had fun

00:45:43.370 --> 00:45:44.830
and yeah I got a million bucks in the bank

00:45:44.830 --> 00:45:45.920
but I'm sad

00:45:45.920 --> 00:45:49.700
'cause I wish I went to the
fucking beach more often.

00:45:49.700 --> 00:45:53.090
So either you're in that
framework or you're not

00:45:53.090 --> 00:45:54.810
which is a great way to put a bow

00:45:54.810 --> 00:45:56.520
on everything I've been telling you.

00:45:56.520 --> 00:45:59.930
You want to blame other
people, the government, Google,

00:45:59.930 --> 00:46:01.310
knock yourself out.

00:46:01.310 --> 00:46:04.453
You will go to the grave
blaming somebody for something.

00:46:05.610 --> 00:46:06.730
You want to understand

00:46:06.730 --> 00:46:08.800
that even if you're in
the worst circumstances,

00:46:08.800 --> 00:46:12.973
the hood, drug parents,
super triple minority,

00:46:14.842 --> 00:46:16.536
trans minority brown,

00:46:16.536 --> 00:46:19.080
everything "stacked against you"

00:46:19.080 --> 00:46:22.680
but the reality of actually
an internet centric world,

00:46:22.680 --> 00:46:26.790
means you have a lot more control
than our grandparents did.

